---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/blog/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { site } from '../config/site';
import { absolute } from '../lib/blog';
import '../styles/blog.css';

const title = 'Privacy Policy | ConnectMouse';
const description =
  'ConnectMouse privacy policy: the phone and computer talk only over your local Wi-Fi. No accounts, no analytics, and no data sent to us or to third parties.';
const effectiveDate = 'November 11, 2026';
const url = absolute(site.links.privacy, Astro.site);
const issues = site.links.issues;

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    name: 'Privacy Policy',
    description,
    url,
    inLanguage: 'en',
    datePublished: '2026-11-11',
    dateModified: '2026-11-11',
    isPartOf: { '@type': 'WebSite', name: site.name, url: absolute('/', Astro.site) },
    about: {
      '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
      name: site.name,
      applicationCategory: 'UtilitiesApplication',
      operatingSystem: 'Android, iOS, macOS 13+, Windows 10/11',
    },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Society Avid' },
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: absolute('/', Astro.site) },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Privacy Policy', item: url },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Header />
  <main id="main" class="blog-page legal-page">
    <div class="blog-bg" aria-hidden="true">
      <span class="hero-blob hero-blob-green"></span>
      <span class="hero-blob hero-blob-blue"></span>
    </div>

    <article class="container legal-wrap">
      <header class="legal-hero">
        <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Privacy Policy' }]} />
        <p class="post-tags">
          <span>Society Avid</span>
          <span class="guide-dot" aria-hidden="true">•</span>
          <span>Effective {effectiveDate}</span>
        </p>
        <h1 class="post-title">Privacy Policy</h1>
        <p class="post-lead">
          How Society Avid handles information in ConnectMouse for Windows, macOS, iOS and Android, and on this
          website.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="prose legal-prose" data-prose>
        <p>
          ConnectMouse is built to work only on your local network. The phone app talks directly to the desktop app
          on your computer. There is no ConnectMouse cloud account, no sign-in, no advertising, and no analytics
          service in the apps. We do not collect, sell or share your personal information.
        </p>

        <h2 id="who-this-covers">Who this policy covers</h2>
        <p>
          This Privacy Policy describes how Society Avid (“we”, “us”) handles information in ConnectMouse, a product
          that includes the ConnectMouse apps for Windows, macOS, iOS and Android, and this website. ConnectMouse
          lets you use your phone as a wireless mouse, touchpad and keyboard for a computer on the same local
          network. The effective date of this policy is {effectiveDate}.
        </p>

        <h2 id="how-it-works">How ConnectMouse works</h2>
        <p>
          When you start the desktop app (the Windows tray app or the macOS menu bar app), it runs a local agent
          that:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            Listens for a WebSocket connection on TCP port <code>8765</code> (by default) on your computer.
          </li>
          <li>
            Announces the computer on your local network with mDNS / DNS-SD (<code>_remotemouse._tcp</code>) on UDP
            port <code>5353</code>, so the phone can find it. That announcement includes the computer’s name,
            operating system type (for example, <code>windows</code> or <code>macos</code>), protocol version and
            local network address.
          </li>
          <li>
            Accepts commands from the phone you connect and injects them locally as mouse and keyboard input (on
            Windows, through the SendInput API; on macOS and other desktops, through the same agent’s input layer).
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          The phone app discovers computers on the same Wi-Fi network or lets you type the computer’s address. It
          then opens a WebSocket to that address on port <code>8765</code> and sends JSON commands (pointer
          movement, clicks, scroll, typed text, shortcuts and media keys). The desktop agent applies those commands
          on the computer. It does not forward them to us or to any other server.
        </p>
        <p>
          You do not need an internet connection for ConnectMouse to work, only that the phone and the computer are
          on the same local network.
        </p>

        <h2 id="information-processed">Information that is processed</h2>
        <p>
          While you use the app, the following information is processed on your devices and, where needed, sent
          between them on your local network:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Input commands from the phone:</strong> pointer movement, clicks, scrolling, text you type in
            the in-app keyboard, keyboard shortcuts and media or volume keys. The desktop agent uses these commands
            only to control the computer in real time. We do not receive this data, and the apps do not keep a
            history of what you typed or which sites you visited.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Connection details on the local network:</strong> the computer’s hostname, local IP address and
            port, and the local IP address of the phone or other device that connects to the agent.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Discovery records</strong> described above (computer name, OS type, protocol version), visible
            to devices on the same local network while the desktop server is running.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The desktop agent may also write brief status messages to the local console or process log, such as when a
          connection is opened or closed (including the connecting device’s local IP address) and periodic counts of
          command types (for example, how many moves, clicks or key events occurred, not the text you typed). These
          messages stay on your computer.
        </p>
        <p>
          On macOS, if you choose <strong>Copy permission diagnostics</strong>, the app copies technical information
          to your clipboard to help with a support request. That information is limited to app and helper paths,
          process identifiers, Accessibility permission results and public code-signature metadata. It is not sent
          automatically. You decide whether to paste it into a bug report.
        </p>

        <h2 id="information-stored">Information stored on your devices</h2>
        <p>ConnectMouse stores a small amount of preference and convenience data only on the device itself:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Phone app (iOS and Android):</strong> whether you have dismissed the first-run tips; your
            touchpad sensitivity; whether the hardware volume keys should control the computer; and the last
            computer you connected to (its local address, and if available its display name and identifier). This is
            stored with the operating system’s local app storage. The phone app follows the language of the device
            and does not store a separate language preference.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Windows app:</strong> your language choice (Portuguese, English or Spanish) and a flag that the
            first-run tray notice has been shown, in the Windows registry under
            <code>HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\RemoteMouse</code>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>macOS app:</strong> the app does not keep its own settings file for language. It follows the
            language of macOS, including any per-app language you set in System Settings.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          You can remove this data by clearing the app’s data or uninstalling the app (and, on Windows, by deleting
          that registry key).
        </p>

        <h2 id="permissions">Permissions the apps request</h2>
        <p>
          The apps request only the system permissions needed to work on the local network and to control the
          computer you have installed them on:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Android:</strong> network access, Wi-Fi state, and multicast (so the phone can discover the
            computer with mDNS). Android also requires the Internet permission for any network socket, including
            connections that stay on the local network. The app is configured to allow cleartext (non-TLS) traffic
            because the local WebSocket is not encrypted, as explained below.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>iOS:</strong> Local Network access and Bonjour for the <code>_remotemouse._tcp</code> service,
            so the phone can find and connect to the computer on your Wi-Fi network.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>macOS:</strong> Local Network access, and Accessibility permission so the agent can move the
            pointer, click and type. The menu bar app will not start the input server until Accessibility is
            granted.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Windows:</strong> the Microsoft Store package declares full-trust desktop privileges
            (<code>runFullTrust</code>) because ConnectMouse is a native Win32 app that injects input and runs a
            local server. It also declares standard local-network capabilities. Firewall rules allow inbound TCP
            <code>8765</code> and UDP <code>5353</code> on private or domain networks.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>The phone app does not use the camera, microphone, location, contacts or photos. It does not scan QR codes.</p>

        <h2 id="what-we-do-not-do">What we do not do</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>We do not create or require an account.</li>
          <li>We do not include analytics, advertising, crash-reporting or telemetry software in the ConnectMouse apps.</li>
          <li>We do not sell or share personal information with third parties, because we do not collect it.</li>
          <li>We do not send your input, contacts, files or browsing activity to the internet or to Society Avid.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          This website is a static marketing site. It does not set analytics or advertising cookies, and it does not
          have user accounts. If you visit the site, the party that hosts it may process ordinary web server logs
          (such as IP address, date and browser type) under that host’s own policy. We do not run a separate
          analytics product on the site.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Windows package may declare an <code>internetClient</code> capability, which Microsoft uses for
          network access in general. The ConnectMouse apps themselves do not contact Society Avid or other online
          services during normal use.
        </p>

        <h2 id="security">Security</h2>
        <div class="callout callout-warning">
          <p>
            Traffic between the phone and the computer travels only on your local network. It is
            <strong>not encrypted</strong>: the connection is a plain WebSocket (<code>ws://</code>), with no TLS,
            and the current version has no pairing code or password. Anyone who can reach TCP port
            <code>8765</code> on that computer while the server is running could send mouse and keyboard commands to
            it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p>
          For that reason, use ConnectMouse on networks you trust, such as your home or office Wi-Fi, do not expose
          port <code>8765</code> to the internet, and stop the desktop server before you join a public or untrusted
          network. Keep the phone and the computer in your control. Uninstalling the apps or stopping the desktop
          server ends the connection.
        </p>

        <h2 id="children">Children’s privacy</h2>
        <p>
          ConnectMouse is not directed at children under 13, and we do not knowingly collect personal information
          from children. Because the apps do not collect personal information in the first place, we do not have a
          children’s profile or a parental sign-in.
        </p>

        <h2 id="changes">Changes to this policy</h2>
        <p>
          We may update this Privacy Policy if the product changes. The effective date at the top of this page will
          change when we do. The current version will always be published at this URL. If a change is material, we
          will also describe it in the project’s release notes or on this website when we can.
        </p>

        <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
        <p>ConnectMouse is published by Society Avid.</p>
        <p>
          We do not publish a support email address. For questions about this policy, or about the product, please
          open an issue in the public repository:{' '}
          <a href={issues} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{issues}</a>. You can also review the source code of the
          apps and the local-network protocol in that repository.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>

<style>
  .legal-wrap {
    max-width: 800px;
    padding-bottom: var(--section-pt);
  }
  .legal-hero {
    padding-bottom: 40px;
  }
  .legal-hero .breadcrumbs {
    margin-bottom: 26px;
  }
  .legal-prose {
    padding-top: 48px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--c-border);
  }
  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .legal-hero {
      padding-bottom: 28px;
    }
    .legal-prose {
      padding-top: 32px;
    }
  }
</style>
